  "imageName": "textformat.size.larger",
  "intro": "Genitive adjectives answer Какого? Какой? Какого? (What kind of?). The key pattern: masculine and neuter share the same ending (-ого/-его), while feminine has its own (-ой/-ей). Remember: -ого is pronounced as '-ova', not '-ogo'! Use genitive adjectives with possession, absence, quantities, and after genitive prepositions.",
  "rules": [
    "Masculine and neuter share identical genitive endings, so you only need to learn one pattern for both.",
    "Pronunciation trap: -ого/-его are pronounced [-ova]/[-eva], not [-ogo]/[-ego]. This applies everywhere in Russian.",
    "Feminine genitive (-ой/-ей) looks identical to dative, instrumental, and prepositional — context tells you which case it is.",
    "Common genitive prepositions to memorize: без, для, до, из, от, около, после, у — all require genitive adjective + noun."
  ],
  "examples": [
    "У меня нет нового телефона. (I don't have a new phone.), masc: -ый → -ого",
    "Это дом старой бабушки. (This is the old grandmother's house.), fem: -ая → -ой",
    "Без хорошего образования трудно. (Without good education it's hard.), neut: -ое → -ого",
    "Из синей коробки. (From the blue box.), fem: -яя → -ей"
  ],
